  it did become at one point so  debilitating that even when friends were  reaching out i just couldn't i couldn't  accept it  i am pregnant with my second child which  we're very excited about i think i feel  more hopeful this time this is almost an  opportunity for a do-over the pregnancy  group that i am in online has been super  helpful  because we're all going through the same  things together no matter how far along  in our pregnancy we are it's really nice  to just be able to just vent those of us  who became  his parents in the pandemic this is all  we know you know i have friends with  children who say they want to get back  to normal but i don't know what it's  like to be a parent not in a pandemic  psychiatrist dr catherine berndorf  specializes in reproductive mental  health at new york presbyterian wild  cornell medical center and joins us to  discuss we're so glad you're here today  to talk about this catherine yesterday  we had simone tate on from poppy seed  health she was talking about as she left  she said maternal health mental health  for women is one of the worst things in  this country that it's under reported  and nobody talks about it it seems like  you were saying the exact same thing  right and thank you guys for doing a  segment like this bringing attention to  something like this that's so important  and under-reported under-diagnosed and  thus under-treated because it's not a  one-size-fits-all what what are you  saying when when you're talking about  maternal mental health well i think it's  the idea around motherhood yeah right i  think that's motherhood is not a  monolithic experience right it affects  people very differently right it's a  life change and i don't think we think  about it that way we we sort of have  romanticized the idea of the baby it's  joyous it's natural and it's easy and no  problem but that's not how women feel  yeah the the popular narratives about  how you have the baby the whole birth  process and then the af